STEP 0 -- baseline, no mirror configured                                  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  <-- the destination port is silent before this case configures anything

STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to receive and read it back"          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction receive
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive                 <-- direction as set
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
  => STEP 1 PASS: the command is accepted and both show forms report the destination, the
     direction and the monitored port correctly.

STEP 2 -- first measurement: nothing copied, and what it was not          done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The mirror reported itself Enabled and correct, yet copied nothing. Everything below was
  checked before concluding anything, because "the DUT is not mirroring" and "the host is not
  capturing" look identical from one empty capture file.
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter               <-- nothing reached eth3 AT ALL, so this is not
                                                 a filter, decode or promiscuity problem
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show interface port1.0.10 status
  port1.0.10  -  connected  1  a-full a-1000  10GBASE-TM     <-- DUT side of the link is up
host# ip -br link show dev eth3
  eth3 UP 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> <-- host side is up
x960-56YSQ#show interface port1.0.10        (before and after a 10-ping burst)
  output packets 86779 ...  ->  output packets 86790          <-- +11 in about 11 s, which
    matches the one-per-second loop-detect frames this bench runs on every port, NOT mirrored
    traffic. The DUT genuinely was not copying.
  An unfiltered capture over 14 s caught not one frame of any kind.
  => STEP 2 done: the absence is real and is on the DUT side.

STEP 3 -- "confirm on the wire that only received traffic is copied"      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the destination port was bounced, which is what makes a re-applied mirror take
  effect on this platform (see the defects below). Nothing else was changed.
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#shutdown
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no shutdown
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  09:02:23.565262 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 1, length 64
  09:02:24.070480 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 2, length 64
  09:02:24.582518 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 3, length 64
  09:02:25.094491 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 4, length 64
  09:02:25.606498 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 5, length 64
  09:02:26.118491 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 6, length 64
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS (what the DUT RECEIVES on port1.0.48) = 6
           echo REPLIES  (what the DUT TRANSMITS out of port1.0.48) = 0
  => STEP 3 PASS: six requests and zero replies -- the exact inverse of the transmit case's
     six replies and zero requests, on the same cabling with the same flow. Only the received
     direction was copied, which is what `direction receive` means. Neither result could be
     produced by a mirror that ignored direction, so each is the other's control. Forwarding
     was undisturbed.

STEP 4 -- defects found while proving the above                           done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A re-applied mirror copies nothing until the destination port is bounced.
  Seven measurements, same flow, ping 6/6 at 0% loss throughout:

    state                                               frames copied
    ------------------------------------------------    -------------
    no mirror (baseline)                                none   correct
    transmit, FIRST EVER apply on this port             YES
    removed, receive applied fresh                      none   <-- defect
    removed, three further fresh applies                none   <-- defect
    receive + destination port bounced                  YES
    removed, transmit applied fresh, no bounce          none   <-- defect
    same, then destination bounced                      YES

  The only mirror that worked without a bounce was the very first one on a port that had never
  been a mirror destination. `show mirror` reported the mirror Enabled with the correct
  direction and source throughout.

  An in-place `direction` change is silently ignored. With `direction receive` live:
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
                                            <-- accepted, no error of any kind
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive                 <-- STILL reports receive, not transmit
  and the capture still showed 6 requests and 0 replies, i.e. receive behaviour. Bouncing the
  port did not help. Only `no mirror interface port1.0.48` followed by a fresh apply changed
  the reported direction, after which `show mirror` read `transmit` and the capture held 6
  replies and 0 requests.
  => STEP 4 done: the running configuration is not always what was typed, and `show mirror`
     agrees with the stale value, so there is no way to detect either defect from the CLI.
     A test that reads back `show mirror` and stops there would record a false pass. That is
     why every verdict in this case rests on captured frames.
